wl-squide/commits/cf8896406ca59719b0343586569a842fcbc66112"}],"sha":"bbfc988c2e136cbf9ffac7f1f6cbfc1a641a7156","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/bbfc988c2e136cbf9ffac7f1f6cbfc1a641a7156"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/794579?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/patricklafrance","id":794579,"login":"patricklafrance","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc5NDU3OQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/cf8896406ca59719b0343586569a842fcbc66112/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-27T03:36:26Z","email":"patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Patrick Lafrance"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-27T03:36:26Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(react-router): sort navigation items at every depth (#688)\n\n* feat(react-router): sort navigation items at every depth\n\n\"$priority\" resolves order at a composition point — somewhere items arrive\nfrom independent modules that cannot see each other, so no author controls the\narray. The top level was the only such place when the prop was designed, which\nis why the sort stopped there.\n\n\"sectionId\" changed that. A module can nest an item into another module''s\nsection, making \"children\" a composition point too. Modules register\nconcurrently (\"Promise.allSettled\" in both LocalModuleRegistry and\nRemoteModuleRegistry) and deferred registrations run after data fetching, so\nthose items are appended in whatever order their registrations complete — a\nfunction of network and data timing rather than authorship. There was no lever\nfor it: array order was nobody''s to set, and \"$priority\" was not consulted.\n\nThe comparator moved out of the hook body into the \"renderItems\" recursion, so\nit applies uniformly. Each array is copied before sorting: for a nested section\nthat array is the registry''s own \"children\", handed over by reference, so\nsorting in place would have reordered the registry and leaked the render order\ninto every other consumer of \"getNavigationItems\". There is a test for that.\n\nBehavior is unchanged for an item with no \"$priority\" below the root — the\ncomparator returns 0 for equal priorities and the sort is stable, so\ndeclaration order survives. It changes for an item carrying an explicit one,\nwhich previously required either the \"sectionId\" option or widening a variable\ninto \"children\", both of which mean a priority was written down and ignored.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: drop the ADR-0026 references\n\n#687 removed the ADR, so the three citations here pointed at nothing. The\ncommit that updated its index row is gone too, since there is no longer a row\nto update.\n\nThe claims themselves stay, they just name the change rather than a record:\n\"root-only before the sort moved into the recursion\" in the design doc, and\n\"this used to be top-level only\" in the skill. Both exist to stop an agent\nrepeating the withdrawn claim that a nested \"$priority\" is ignored, which does\nnot depend on an ADR existing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore: stop linting a git worktree checked out inside the repo\n\n\"globalIgnores\" patterns are anchored at the repo root, so a worktree living at\n\".claude/worktrees/<name>\" brings its own \"packages\", \"docs\" and\n\"skills-lock.json\" which escape every existing entry and get linted against the\nroot config. They then fail on rules the packages configure for themselves\n(\"react-hooks/refs\", \"testing-library/render-result-naming-convention\" reported\nas not found) plus 68 \"jsonc/indent\" warnings from a nested \"skills-lock.json\".\n\nRoot lint went from failing to clean with this one entry, which also confirms\nnothing outside the worktree had a problem.\n\nThis blocks committing, not just CI: \".claude/hooks/pre-commit.sh\" runs\n\"turbo run lint --continue\", so any session with a worktree there cannot commit\nat all. CI never saw it because the directory does not exist on a runner.\n\nUnrelated to the rest of this PR, and included because the hook gates every\ncommit on it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct the three places that still scoped the sort to the top level\n\nAll three from review, and all the same miss: the prose docs were updated and\nthe source comments describing the same behavior were not.\n\n- \"NavigationItemRegistry.ts\" carried \"Only a menu''s top-level items are sorted\n  by Squide; at any other depth the value is forwarded to the code rendering the\n  menu, which sorts if it wants to\" on the exported \"$priority\". That is the\n  highest-visibility spot in the repo, since it is what an IDE shows on hover\n  and what an agent reading the type believes, and it stated the opposite of the\n  shipped behavior. \"NavigationSection.$priority\" inherits it by reference.\n- \"useRenderedNavigationItems.tsx\" told the renderer it should sort a section''s\n  items itself, which is exactly what this change removes the need for.\n- \"docs/reference/routing/useNavigationItems.md\" is a fifth public page, missed\n  because it describes the sort by cross-referencing the hook rather than\n  documenting it.\n\nSwept the rest rather than fixing only what was reported: no other stale\n\"top-level\" or \"unsorted\" claim remains in the react-router sources, \"docs\",\n\"agent-docs\" or \"agent-skills\". \"useNavigationItemsByMenu.md\" is correct as\nwritten, it only claims that hook does not sort.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: the escape hatch published in the changeset does not work\n\nReview is right, and this is the worst of the findings because it is a\nmigration path published into two CHANGELOGs for a change that alters rendered\noutput.\n\n\"renderItems\" sorts every array it receives, the root one included, so\npre-sorting the tree and handing it over cannot produce declaration order. The\ncaller''s order is discarded and only ties survive, since the sort is stable.\nVerified against the real comparator:\n\n    declared order:               A,B,C\n    caller pre-sorts to declared: A,B,C  -> hook output: B,A,C\n    caller pre-sorts reversed:    C,B,A  -> hook output: B,A,C\n    stripped of \"$priority\":      A,B,C  -> hook output: A,B,C\n\nEven a reversed pre-sort lands on the same output. Removing \"$priority\" from\nthe tree, or not using the hook, is the only thing that works. Corrected in the\nchangeset, the public doc and the skill''s runtime-api reference.\n\nAlso merged \"main\", which moved under this branch. #692 re-synced\n\"patterns.md\" from the docs and re-added the \"sortByPriority\" recipe that this\nPR removes, so the merged file read as its own contradiction: the corrected\nparagraph followed by a block telling the reader to pre-sort, plus the dead\nrecipe. Removed, and the trailing \"always default it when comparing\" note kept\nbut pointed at the \"priority\" render prop, which is where a consumer still\nwrites a comparator.\n\nSkill bumped to 1.22, since #692 already published 1.21.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(react-router)!: release the every-depth sort as a major\n\nThe previous behavior was not merely undocumented, it was promised: \"An item\nnested inside a section renders in the order it appears in that section''s\nchildren array, and its $priority is ignored\". Removing a stated contract is a\nmajor, and a caret update should not silently reorder a production menu.\n\nRewrote the changeset as a breaking-change entry: who is affected (an explicit\n\"$priority\" at depth >= 1, reachable three ways, all of which previously\naccepted and ignored it), who is not (no nested priority means the comparator is\na no-op and declaration order survives), and what to do.\n\nThe migration section spells out that pre-sorting the tree does not preserve a\ncaller''s order, since the hook sorts every array it receives. Removing\n\"$priority\" from the tree, or not using the hook, are the only options. That was\nthe escape hatch this PR got wrong once already, so it is worth being explicit\nabout in the published changelog.\n\n\"@squide/react-router\" goes to 10.0.0 and \"@squide/firefly\" to 19.0.0.\n\"firefly-module-federation\" and \"firefly-storybook\" take the patch bumps that\n\"updateInternalDependencies: patch\" prescribes for dependents.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test: pin the root array against an in-place sort\n\nThe \"sorting does not mutate the items it was given\" test asserted on a\none-element root array, which no sort can reorder. It passed identically against\n\"items.sort(byPriority)\" and \"[...items].sort(byPriority)\", so only the children\nassertion was doing any work. Confirmed:\n\n    old fixture / correct -> [\"Section\"]              PASSES\n    old fixture / mutated -> [\"Section\"]              PASSES\n    new fixture / correct -> [\"Section\",\"Root high\"]  PASSES\n    new fixture / mutated -> [\"Root high\",\"Section\"]  FAILS\n\nThe root array now holds two elements whose priorities would swap them, so it\npins the root the way the children assertion already pinned the nested array.\nThat also explains the single kill this test reported in the earlier mutation\nrun rather than two.\n\nAlso dropped \"top-level\" from a present-tense comment about the 0 default. The\ntwo remaining mentions in the file are correct: one is explicitly past tense\nabout the pre-change behavior, the other compares a section''s sort to the\ntop-level one rather than claiming the sort stops there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: the escape hatch has to rebuild the tree, not edit it\n\n\"Remove $priority from the tree you pass in\" is a trap as written, and this PR\nsupplies the proof: \"useNavigationItems\" returns the registry''s own objects, a\nsection''s \"children\" is the registry''s own array, and the\n\"sorting does not mutate the items it was given\" test exists precisely because\nof that.\n\nSo the obvious reading of my own advice corrupts the registry for every other\nconsumer, and a shallow copy silently only half works. Measured:\n\n    shallow copy  -> children sort High,Low   (wanted Low,High)\n                     children === registry children: true\n    deep rebuild  -> children sort Low,High   registry untouched\n\nThis matters more than it would otherwise because the same PR removes the old\n\"sortByPriority\" recipe, so this is the only escape hatch on offer.\n\nAll four places now say to build a new tree and show the recursive map, with the\nreturn type annotated since the helper reports TS7023 without it. Compile\nchecked against the real \"NavigationItem\" type.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9e0df2d362df328f140acb54f624dc081d1a7aa1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/trees/9e0df2d362df328f140acb54f624dc081d1a7aa1"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/commits/cf8896406ca59719b0343586569a842fcbc66112","verification":{"payload":"tree 9e0df2d362df328f140acb54f624dc081d1a7aa1\nparent 65ea7c630f03858e5fdecd6e0ebccef49f37d92e\nauthor Patrick Lafrance <patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com> 1787801786 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787801786 -0400\n\nfeat(react-router): sort navigation items at every depth (#688)\n\n* feat(react-router): sort navigation items at every depth\n\n\"$priority\" resolves order at a composition point — somewhere items arrive\nfrom independent modules that cannot see each other, so no author controls the\narray. The top level was the only such place when the prop was designed, which\nis why the sort stopped there.\n\n\"sectionId\" changed that. A module can nest an item into another module''s\nsection, making \"children\" a composition point too. Modules register\nconcurrently (\"Promise.allSettled\" in both LocalModuleRegistry and\nRemoteModuleRegistry) and deferred registrations run after data fetching, so\nthose items are appended in whatever order their registrations complete — a\nfunction of network and data timing rather than authorship. There was no lever\nfor it: array order was nobody''s to set, and \"$priority\" was not consulted.\n\nThe comparator moved out of the hook body into the \"renderItems\" recursion, so\nit applies uniformly. Each array is copied before sorting: for a nested section\nthat array is the registry''s own \"children\", handed over by reference, so\nsorting in place would have reordered the registry and leaked the render order\ninto every other consumer of \"getNavigationItems\". There is a test for that.\n\nBehavior is unchanged for an item with no \"$priority\" below the root — the\ncomparator returns 0 for equal priorities and the sort is stable, so\ndeclaration order survives. It changes for an item carrying an explicit one,\nwhich previously required either the \"sectionId\" option or widening a variable\ninto \"children\", both of which mean a priority was written down and ignored.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: drop the ADR-0026 references\n\n#687 removed the ADR, so the three citations here pointed at nothing. The\ncommit that updated its index row is gone too, since there is no longer a row\nto update.\n\nThe claims themselves stay, they just name the change rather than a record:\n\"root-only before the sort moved into the recursion\" in the design doc, and\n\"this used to be top-level only\" in the skill. Both exist to stop an agent\nrepeating the withdrawn claim that a nested \"$priority\" is ignored, which does\nnot depend on an ADR existing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore: stop linting a git worktree checked out inside the repo\n\n\"globalIgnores\" patterns are anchored at the repo root, so a worktree living at\n\".claude/worktrees/<name>\" brings its own \"packages\", \"docs\" and\n\"skills-lock.json\" which escape every existing entry and get linted against the\nroot config. They then fail on rules the packages configure for themselves\n(\"react-hooks/refs\", \"testing-library/render-result-naming-convention\" reported\nas not found) plus 68 \"jsonc/indent\" warnings from a nested \"skills-lock.json\".\n\nRoot lint went from failing to clean with this one entry, which also confirms\nnothing outside the worktree had a problem.\n\nThis blocks committing, not just CI: \".claude/hooks/pre-commit.sh\" runs\n\"turbo run lint --continue\", so any session with a worktree there cannot commit\nat all. CI never saw it because the directory does not exist on a runner.\n\nUnrelated to the rest of this PR, and included because the hook gates every\ncommit on it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct the three places that still scoped the sort to the top level\n\nAll three from review, and all the same miss: the prose docs were updated and\nthe source comments describing the same behavior were not.\n\n- \"NavigationItemRegistry.ts\" carried \"Only a menu''s top-level items are sorted\n  by Squide; at any other depth the value is forwarded to the code rendering the\n  menu, which sorts if it wants to\" on the exported \"$priority\". That is the\n  highest-visibility spot in the repo, since it is what an IDE shows on hover\n  and what an agent reading the type believes, and it stated the opposite of the\n  shipped behavior. \"NavigationSection.$priority\" inherits it by reference.\n- \"useRenderedNavigationItems.tsx\" told the renderer it should sort a section''s\n  items itself, which is exactly what this change removes the need for.\n- \"docs/reference/routing/useNavigationItems.md\" is a fifth public page, missed\n  because it describes the sort by cross-referencing the hook rather than\n  documenting it.\n\nSwept the rest rather than fixing only what was reported: no other stale\n\"top-level\" or \"unsorted\" claim remains in the react-router sources, \"docs\",\n\"agent-docs\" or \"agent-skills\". \"useNavigationItemsByMenu.md\" is correct as\nwritten, it only claims that hook does not sort.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: the escape hatch published in the changeset does not work\n\nReview is right, and this is the worst of the findings because it is a\nmigration path published into two CHANGELOGs for a change that alters rendered\noutput.\n\n\"renderItems\" sorts every array it receives, the root one included, so\npre-sorting the tree and handing it over cannot produce declaration order. The\ncaller''s order is discarded and only ties survive, since the sort is stable.\nVerified against the real comparator:\n\n    declared order:               A,B,C\n    caller pre-sorts to declared: A,B,C  -> hook output: B,A,C\n    caller pre-sorts reversed:    C,B,A  -> hook output: B,A,C\n    stripped of \"$priority\":      A,B,C  -> hook output: A,B,C\n\nEven a reversed pre-sort lands on the same output. Removing \"$priority\" from\nthe tree, or not using the hook, is the only thing that works. Corrected in the\nchangeset, the public doc and the skill''s runtime-api reference.\n\nAlso merged \"main\", which moved under this branch. #692 re-synced\n\"patterns.md\" from the docs and re-added the \"sortByPriority\" recipe that this\nPR removes, so the merged file read as its own contradiction: the corrected\nparagraph followed by a block telling the reader to pre-sort, plus the dead\nrecipe. Removed, and the trailing \"always default it when comparing\" note kept\nbut pointed at the \"priority\" render prop, which is where a consumer still\nwrites a comparator.\n\nSkill bumped to 1.22, since #692 already published 1.21.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(react-router)!: release the every-depth sort as a major\n\nThe previous behavior was not merely undocumented, it was promised: \"An item\nnested inside a section renders in the order it appears in that section''s\nchildren array, and its $priority is ignored\". Removing a stated contract is a\nmajor, and a caret update should not silently reorder a production menu.\n\nRewrote the changeset as a breaking-change entry: who is affected (an explicit\n\"$priority\" at depth >= 1, reachable three ways, all of which previously\naccepted and ignored it), who is not (no nested priority means the comparator is\na no-op and declaration order survives), and what to do.\n\nThe migration section spells out that pre-sorting the tree does not preserve a\ncaller''s order, since the hook sorts every array it receives. Removing\n\"$priority\" from the tree, or not using the hook, are the only options. That was\nthe escape hatch this PR got wrong once already, so it is worth being explicit\nabout in the published changelog.\n\n\"@squide/react-router\" goes to 10.0.0 and \"@squide/firefly\" to 19.0.0.\n\"firefly-module-federation\" and \"firefly-storybook\" take the patch bumps that\n\"updateInternalDependencies: patch\" prescribes for dependents.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test: pin the root array against an in-place sort\n\nThe \"sorting does not mutate the items it was given\" test asserted on a\none-element root array, which no sort can reorder. It passed identically against\n\"items.sort(byPriority)\" and \"[...items].sort(byPriority)\", so only the children\nassertion was doing any work. Confirmed:\n\n    old fixture / correct -> [\"Section\"]              PASSES\n    old fixture / mutated -> [\"Section\"]              PASSES\n    new fixture / correct -> [\"Section\",\"Root high\"]  PASSES\n    new fixture / mutated -> [\"Root high\",\"Section\"]  FAILS\n\nThe root array now holds two elements whose priorities would swap them, so it\npins the root the way the children assertion already pinned the nested array.\nThat also explains the single kill this test reported in the earlier mutation\nrun rather than two.\n\nAlso dropped \"top-level\" from a present-tense comment about the 0 default. The\ntwo remaining mentions in the file are correct: one is explicitly past tense\nabout the pre-change behavior, the other compares a section''s sort to the\ntop-level one rather than claiming the sort stops there.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: the escape hatch has to rebuild the tree, not edit it\n\n\"Remove $priority from the tree you pass in\" is a trap as written, and this PR\nsupplies the proof: \"useNavigationItems\" returns the registry''s own objects, a\nsection''s \"children\" is the registry''s own array, and the\n\"sorting does not mutate the items it was given\" test exists precisely because\nof that.\n\nSo the obvious reading of my own advice corrupts the registry for every other\nconsumer, and a shallow copy silently only half works. Measured:\n\n    shallow copy  -> children sort High,Low   (wanted Low,High)\n                     children === registry children: true\n    deep rebuild  -> children sort Low,High   registry untouched\n\nThis matters more than it would otherwise because the same PR removes the old\n\"sortByPriority\" recipe, so this is the only escape hatch on offer.\n\nAll four places now say to build a new tree and show the recursive map, with the\nreturn type annotated since the helper reports TS7023 without it. Compile\nchecked against the real \"NavigationItem\" type.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqj7C6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA3F8QACc6Oid2w1xeet/snsX3w8WC\nsity0uNFfrhI0MazL6yNoO8GaLb/hLGMd7zPJCUiy+U5sHBD5H5FAXTkq5LpjFuc\nvFyMQVLpFnByVFgFxRlaJJwbP7k6L0BpkYI026peq6LjviGgh2iOPiQSx/jkEQtR\nRaaXT95fSbwuSIxXXAN1HybFfkPwjRiVNj8wc+iZNm5IJAK8SgzF5NtD9IJv5Q64\nw+rVjzkIb6tjebrX7KZcuklpK4MizqD0CSuFttH5wXXkBKfoJNugry8MmICKf0e1\nP/awKuBud/SCegmPB9lk5g/xqeMDEwYMdR3Gqhu8tDFPMm2fG0Q2JDW/rrdNj3b7\nRvwt6NypgpOm//ZGABrXNkGpRxmeD678RjlsqN9IGPtgymgCJLGY9q8tSSEZbzbR\nAbcLCCdOiUxj7wEWDoRkhEBlwFfrjm7acZ+mZsQqRabvKPWRqiOf+vXEEVcXaB7j\nePPBI6AR+lOTWVzb6EGYLb/vxP/5aA42f96JFPbfml59zR3JOy3ZSXyYb4XTFDbo\npraDz2acRRG0wikM6EcIpeKpMj4zu6sVLmJ2nDkjktOmVwBI41veCxzagMmqo6Z4\n0XjoLcFzf5L4HaXcLERQx4XAw8AP23rZkXOQzgLOSae0amOvYznB8B1ZCM9Tj4au\nxihKyHi+zWezNuFs+bbR\n=Aq/M\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-27T03:36:27Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/us