m> 1787796547 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787796547 -0400\n\nci(changesets): version packages (#691)\n\nCo-authored-by: github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqj5xDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4koQACBFrOGwUZZpLwx81VQQyyD6\ndOWSk74FL5rVXwanfz9VUf1b+BBSi/H6AqvzQeK/rTuRM7/aLhsbR9Ok6rE7Omh6\nGf3KAR8LhdcvZMrxOw+qF/cC2NDl9o5V+42AsIfojJqqTcCNKWkmzkxAYJDFEgBY\nxjglu9GNqUK6vog8pJXiQhg9zEaVgGGVfw48eZrkhX/tRna5pPbXKjR+7lOxMeN6\nEu7XeoqjDt2rV+rEbwZVMVHE5eLVVj5tHr8Pxxo/bSi31pIthXE/osN3fM3tlcvt\nmcNc7JwC4APH/pGJA8wCetqClmTYtu0haxvUwDFjd0wua91RVeojbIPZtukBB7Ur\nkEbCZ0jTo72XqkrIQr8Lb/42j6Kdpzaax72h5ogmu8Cqqx6/ECHFvJGcja8TjzCe\neMYBbqsz8rgXf4hECnEjFV+yUHXxIFDrrnsiubhrpQpAefgAyENxkMMTQFAiguKY\nlO7ktxA+nvb311CKLoIZeV42bKn2m+CqXJ3dzwfuOrv/yAl/qDYG6RvCSegPQSwG\nEs1E3VZokngUFB4sZE1DyTlCvVwaRx/qjiDMsK8D5bYpHmOAVyxy6UGJssK9UalA\nAJScRbJVwP5euZbfIO+WeW91lRtQPPZKFUkPHDBdIN0veMZsyCj9ygD/VxIttJ2X\n2p5bY5D33fN/xXkRY7Eg\n=9w0P\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-27T02:09:08Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/workleap/wl-squide/commit/36d9ae0b978c7697aa0a44880c351c0e959209ca","node_id":"C_kwDOJbZi_NoAKDM2ZDlhZTBiOTc4Yzc2OTdhYTBhNDQ4ODBjMzUxYzBlOTU5MjA5Y2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/workleap/wl-squide/commit/b89b044c5989ea4f16a8424a0886fdaf6181ce20","sha":"b89b044c5989ea4f16a8424a0886fdaf6181ce20","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/b89b044c5989ea4f16a8424a0886fdaf6181ce20"}],"sha":"36d9ae0b978c7697aa0a44880c351c0e959209ca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/36d9ae0b978c7697aa0a44880c351c0e959209ca"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/794579?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/patricklafrance","id":794579,"login":"patricklafrance","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc5NDU3OQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/b89b044c5989ea4f16a8424a0886fdaf6181ce20/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-27T02:02:46Z","email":"patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Patrick Lafrance"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-27T02:02:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(react-router): hand an item $priority to the code rendering the menu (#687)\n\n* fix(react-router): forward an item $priority to the code rendering the menu\n\n\"$priority\" has two jobs: Squide sorts a menu''s top-level items with it, and\nit is handed to the code rendering the menu so that code can order a section''s\nitems itself. The second one stopped working.\n\n\"toLinkProps\" used to spread a link''s remaining props into \"linkProps\"\nuntouched, so \"$priority\" reached the renderer as \"linkProps.$priority\". #665\nintroduced \"stripMetadataProps\" to keep \"$\" prefixed props off the DOM element,\nwhich is right, and \"$priority\" went out with them. \"toMenuProps\" never\nforwarded it at all. A renderer had no way to read a nested item''s priority.\n\n- \"NavigationLinkRenderProps\" and \"NavigationSectionRenderProps\" gain a\n  \"priority?: number\", forwarded as declared so an unset priority stays\n  distinguishable from an explicit 0. The top-level sort keeps its 0 default.\n- \"$priority\" moves from \"RootNavigationItem\" onto \"NavigationLink\" and\n  \"NavigationSection\", making it legal at any depth. Writing one in a\n  \"children\" literal was a TS2353 error even though the registry has always\n  carried a nested item''s \"$priority\" through verbatim.\n- \"RootNavigationItem\" stays as an alias of \"NavigationItem\".\n\nSorting behavior is unchanged: the hook sorts the array it receives and\nrecurses into \"children\" in declaration order.\n\nThe docs said \"$priority\" was scoped to top-level items and ignored elsewhere,\nwhich described the regression rather than the design. Corrected in the four\npublic docs, the design doc and the skill.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(adr): record that $priority is forwarded, not enforced\n\nThree attempts in a row read the top-level-only sort as a bug and tried to\neither finish it or delete the prop from nested items. Nothing said why the\nvalue is on a nested item, so this writes it down: Squide sorts a menu''s\ntop-level items because they come from independent modules that cannot see\neach other; inside a section the declaring module already owns the order, so\nsorting there would silently reorder live menus with no consumer opt-in.\n\nAlso records why the \"$priority: never\" approach in #685 is not expressible:\nrefusing the property rejects every variable typed as a navigation item, since\nsuch a variable carries an optional \"$priority\" whether or not one was set.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: fix the sortByPriority snippet and stop naming renderItem as an ordering lever\n\nFive findings from a review pass:\n\n- The \"sortByPriority\" helper in \"useRenderedNavigationItems.md\" is recursive\n  with no return type annotation, so it reports TS7023/TS7024 rather than being\n  inferred. It was the only code the docs offered for the headline use case.\n  Annotated, and made the snippet self-contained — it used\n  \"NavigationLink\", \"NavigationSection\", \"useRenderedNavigationItems\",\n  \"renderItem\" and \"renderSection\" without importing any of them. Verified the\n  corrected form compiles.\n- ADR-0026 and the skill both listed \"read priority in renderItem\" as a way to\n  order a section. It is not: \"RenderItemFunction\" is\n  \"(item, key, index, level)\" and sees one item at a time, and \"renderSection\"\n  receives elements that are already rendered. Pre-sorting the tree is the only\n  lever. The public doc had this right; the two agent-facing files did not.\n- Documented the \"?? 0\" trap on the render side. A bare\n  \"y.priority - x.priority\" yields NaN against an unprioritized item and the\n  sort silently no-ops. Forwarding stays raw, this belongs in the docs.\n- Restored \"RootNavigationItem\" in the two hook docs. The signatures return it\n  and it is what a consumer sees on hover, so naming only the alias target was\n  a mismatch with the IDE.\n- Added two tests for the \"sectionId\" nesting path, static and pending. Nothing\n  rebuilds the item so the \"$priority\" survives, but nothing guarded it either,\n  which is the shape of gap that let the forwarding regress.\n\nreact-router 247/247, typecheck 35/35, lint 71/71.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: address review on the $priority forwarding\n\n- `useNavigationItems.md` told the reader to sort by `$priority` without\n  documenting it as a property of the returned items. Both sibling pages got\n  the bullet in this PR; this one is now consistent with them.\n- `hooks-api.md` already had a render-props table further down the file. The\n  paragraph I added duplicated it and the table still omitted `priority`.\n  Dropped the paragraph, put `priority` in the table.\n- Recounted the Retype line-highlight range on the `sortByPriority` snippet.\n  It was set before the snippet grew its imports, so it pointed at the import\n  block instead of the helper and the line applying it.\n\nDeclined: the singular-verb agreement in the `FireflyRuntime.md` sorting\nbullets and \"Could either by\" in the property lists are pre-existing prose,\nunrelated to this change, and appear in several files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct what a NaN comparator actually does\n\n\"the sort silently no-ops\" was wrong twice over.\n\nA comparator returning NaN is coerced to +0 per CompareArrayElements, so it\nreads as \"these two are equal\". That makes the comparator inconsistent and the\nitems come back partially reordered — not left alone. A reader debugging a\nscrambled menu would have looked in the wrong place.\n\nIt is also not silent: \"priority\" is optional, so \"y.priority - x.priority\" is\na TS18048 error before it ever runs. The type system is the first line of\ndefense here, which is worth saying rather than hiding.\n\nCorrected in the public doc and in the skill, where an agent would have\nrepeated the claim.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(adr): the numbering rule has to account for in-flight records\n\n\"Take the highest in this folder and increment\" only sees \"main\", so it hands\nout a number another open PR already claimed. It has now produced a wrong\nreview finding three times, twice on this PR alone: 0022 belongs to the open\n#684 and 0023-0025 to the \"spike/registry-projection\" branch, none of which\nthis folder shows.\n\nNumbers are claimed when an ADR is written, not when it merges. The rule now\nsays to check unmerged branches and gives the command, and says explicitly\nthat a gap is the expected outcome rather than a mistake to correct — the gap\nis what stops two PRs from colliding on the same number.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: this is a feature, not a regression fix\n\nI claimed #665 removed an intended forwarding channel. It did not, and the\nhistory says so plainly.\n\n\"a39c15176\", the first version of this package, sorted the array it received\nand then stripped the property before recursing:\n\n    .sort(/* by priority */)\n    // priority is intentionally omitted.\n    .map(({ priority, ...itemProps }) => ({ ...itemProps }));\n\nThat strip survived two years and every refactor — \"git show 7925636fb^\" has\nit verbatim, comment included, immediately before the \"$meta\" PR. #665 removed\nthe strip and put \"stripMetadataProps\" in its place. There was never a\nforwarding channel to break.\n\nThe only way \"$priority\" ever reached a renderer was as a stray key inside\n\"linkProps\", and only on nested items, since the root-level strip did not apply\nto \"children\". From there it was spread onto the DOM Link as an invalid\n\"$priority\" attribute. That is the leak \"stripMetadataProps\" was written to\nclose, not a feature it removed.\n\nSo the changeset, the ADR and the test comment all described a regression that\ndid not happen. Rewritten.\n\nThe real reason this change is wanted is in the rewritten ADR: the root-only\nscope was coherent while a section''s \"children\" were always one array literal\nauthored in one place. The \"sectionId\" option made \"children\" a cross-module\ncomposition point, which is precisely what \"$priority\" exists for at the root,\nand did not revisit the sort. Modules register concurrently, so the resulting\norder is a function of network timing rather than authorship, with no lever to\ncorrect it.\n\nADR-0026 now records the whole decision — sort at every depth *and* forward the\nvalue — rather than a position already superseded before it landed. Renamed to\nmatch. The sort ships separately so a behavior change is not bundled with an\nadditive one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(adr): the index row must not claim behavior this PR does not ship\n\nThe row said \"$priority\" orders items at every depth. This PR only forwards\nthe value; the sort lands separately, which the ADR itself says. index.md\ndescribes itself as the quick reference where each line stands alone, and\nCLAUDE.md points agents at it before an API change, so an overstated row here\nmakes an agent assert the thing the rest of the PR carefully withdraws. The\ndesign doc updated in this same PR said the opposite.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(adr): drop ADR-0026\n\nNot needed. The change is a forwarding fix plus a type move, both of which the\nchangeset and the code comments already explain, and the archaeology that\njustified a record is in the commit history where it is discoverable.\n\nRemoves the ADR, its index row, and the changeset''s reference to it. Verified no\nreference remains in \".changeset\", \"agent-docs\", \"docs\", \"agent-skills\" or the\nreact-router sources and tests. \"agent-docs/adr/index.md\" ends at 0020 again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7c2134b5fdb63657a8e17becd9377b226e3d1805","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/trees/7c2134b5fdb63657a8e17becd9377b226e3d1805"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/commits/b89b044c5989ea4f16a8424a0886fdaf6181ce20","verification":{"payload":"tree 7c2134b5fdb63657a8e17becd9377b226e3d1805\nparent 2c64a16002c3cbe75078daaf42640326ff114aa1\nauthor Patrick Lafrance <patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com> 1787796166 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787796166 -0400\n\nfeat(react-router): hand an item $priority to the code rendering the menu (#687)\n\n* fix(react-router): forward an item $priority to the code rendering the menu\n\n\"$priority\" has two jobs: Squide sorts a menu''s top-level items with it, and\nit is handed to the code rendering the menu so that code can order a section''s\nitems itself. The second one stopped working.\n\n\"toLinkProps\" used to spread a link''s remaining props into \"linkProps\"\nuntouched, so \"$priority\" reached the renderer as \"linkProps.$priority\". #665\nintroduced \"stripMetadataProps\" to keep \"$\" prefixed props off the DOM element,\nwhich is right, and \"$priority\" went out with them. \"toMenuProps\" never\nforwarded it at all. A renderer had no way to read a nested item''s priority.\n\n- \"NavigationLinkRenderProps\" and \"NavigationSectionRenderProps\" gain a\n  \"priority?: number\", forwarded as declared so an unset priority stays\n  distinguishable from an explicit 0. The top-level sort keeps its 0 default.\n- \"$priority\" moves from \"RootNavigationItem\" onto \"NavigationLink\" and\n  \"NavigationSection\", making it legal at any depth. Writing one in a\n  \"children\" literal was a TS2353 error even though the registry has always\n  carried a nested item''s \"$priority\" through verbatim.\n- \"RootNavigationItem\" stays as an alias of \"NavigationItem\".\n\nSorting behavior is unchanged: the hook sorts the array it receives and\nrecurses into \"children\" in declaration order.\n\nThe docs said \"$priority\" was scoped to top-level items and ignored elsewhere,\nwhich described the regression rather than the design. Corrected in the four\npublic docs, the design doc and the skill.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(adr): record that $priority is forwarded, not enforced\n\nThree attempts in a row read the top-level-only sort as a bug and tried to\neither finish it or delete the prop from nested items. Nothing said why the\nvalue is on a nested item, so this writes it down: Squide sorts a menu''s\ntop-level items because they come from independent modules that cannot see\neach other; inside a section the declaring module already owns the order, so\nsorting there would silently reorder live menus with no consumer opt-in.\n\nAlso records why the \"$priority: never\" approach in #685 is not expressible:\nrefusing the property rejects every variable typed as a navigation item, since\nsuch a variable carries an optional \"$priority\" whether or not one was set.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: fix the sortByPriority snippet and stop naming renderItem as an ordering lever\n\nFive findings from a review pass:\n\n- The \"sortByPriority\" helper in \"useRenderedNavigationItems.md\" is recursive\n  with no return type annotation, so it reports TS7023/TS7024 rather than being\n  inferred. It was the only code the docs offered for the headline use case.\n  Annotated, and made the snippet self-contained — it used\n  \"NavigationLink\", \"NavigationSection\", \"useRenderedNavigationItems\",\n  \"renderItem\" and \"renderSection\" without importing any of them. Verified the\n  corrected form compiles.\n- ADR-0026 and the skill both listed \"read priority