om/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/2c64a16002c3cbe75078daaf42640326ff114aa1"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/794579?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/patricklafrance","id":794579,"login":"patricklafrance","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc5NDU3OQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/779bb3f05b6b711ebb88f2f49d470109e93aa34b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-27T01:47:12Z","email":"patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Patrick Lafrance"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-27T01:47:12Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(react-router): length-prefix the section index key (#689)\n\n* fix(react-router): separate the section index key halves unambiguously\n\nThe section index is keyed by a \"menuId\" and a section \"$id\" joined into one\nstring with a \"-\". Both halves are consumer-provided and either can contain a\n\"-\", so (\"analytics\", \"sidebar-performance\") and (\"analytics-sidebar\",\n\"performance\") produced the same key and shared one index entry.\n\nThat put a nested item in the wrong menu, made registering the second of two\ncolliding sections throw \"A navigation section index has already been\nregistered for the menu\", and let strict mode report one missing section when\ntwo were missing.\n\nThe separator is now NUL, which cannot appear in a \"menuId\" or a section \"$id\"\nanyone would write. Written as an escape rather than a literal control byte, so\nthe file stays plain ASCII and grep does not treat it as binary.\n\n\"parseSectionIndexKey\" splits on the new separator. It stays deprecated because\nthe key format is an implementation detail, but its deprecation note no longer\nclaims the key is unrecoverable — it now returns the correct pair for ids\ncontaining a \"-\", which it could not do before.\n\nTwo existing tests hardcoded \"foo-bar\" as an index key, which is the coupling\nthat deprecation warns against. They now read the pair off the pending items,\nwhere \"menuId\" and \"sectionId\" are separate fields.\n\nThe new tests use the canonical colliding pair rather than random ids. Randomly\ngenerated ids essentially never construct a collision, so a fuzz over a hostile\nalphabet passes against the unfixed code and proves nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(react-router): length-prefix the section index key\n\nReview was right that a separator alone is not enough. Nothing constrains a\n\"menuId\" or a section \"$id\" to exclude whichever character is picked, so a\nseparator only moves the collision rather than removing it. A differential over\neight pairs: the \"-\" separator collides twice, and a NUL separator still\ncollides once, on (\"a\" + NUL + \"b\", \"c\") against (\"a\", \"b\" + NUL + \"c\").\nLength-prefixing the menu id collides zero times and round-trips exactly,\nincluding for a menu id that itself starts with a digit and a NUL.\n\nThis is the form that shipped in \"@squide/react-router@9.3.0\" before the 9.4.0\nrevert pulled it back out, so this re-lands it rather than inventing a weaker\nvariant. It answers the \"the invariant is not enforced\" finding by removing the\ninvariant, not by validating input and throwing, which would add a runtime\nfailure mode for ids nobody writes.\n\nAlso from review:\n\n- \"parseSectionIndexKey\" is a public export whose behavior changes here and had\n  no test. Three round-trip tests now drive a key out of the registry and parse\n  it back, covering a \"-\" in an id, the separator in an id, and an empty menu\n  id. They are also the only thing tying it to the private\n  \"createSectionIndexKey\", which nothing else pins.\n- Three comments still described the key as \"menuId-sectionId\". They now name\n  the key builder instead of a literal format.\n- Two tests indexed into a pending registrations array without asserting it was\n  non-empty, so an unexpected empty result failed on a TypeError rather than an\n  assertion. They capture the array and assert its length first.\n- Bumped to minor. The identical fix shipped as a minor in 9.3.0, and the\n  \"getPendingSectionIds\" format change should not reach a consumer pinned to\n  \"~9.4.0\" silently.\n\nDeclined: supporting both the old and new key formats in\n\"parseSectionIndexKey\". Keys are produced and consumed within one bootstrap, so\na key outliving the version that made it is not a real case, and accepting two\nformats reintroduces the ambiguity being removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: state what parseSectionIndexKey actually does with an old-format key\n\nThe changeset said it returns a single element. That was true of the \"split\"\nimplementation it was written against, and I changed the parse to a length\nprefix without re-reading the prose.\n\nAn old-format key holds no separator, so \"indexOf\" is -1, the length prefix\nparses to NaN, and both slices coerce it to 0. Verified:\n\n    parseSectionIndexKey(\"analytics-sidebar-performance\")\n    // [\"\", \"analytics-sidebar-performance\"]\n\nA two-element array with an empty menu id and the whole input as the section\nid, which is worse than a single element because it has the shape of a valid\nresult. Worth stating precisely, since this text is published into two\nCHANGELOG files.\n\nThe behavior itself is unchanged from the implementation released in 9.3.0 and\nthe input is outside the function contract, so this corrects the description\nrather than adding a guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(react-router): make the no-separator path in parseSectionIndexKey explicit\n\nThe path was accidental and my own changeset described it wrongly, which is a\nbug I introduced while fixing the previous review finding.\n\nWithout a guard, an input holding no separator makes \"indexOf\" return -1, so\n\"slice(0, separatorIndex)\" reads the whole input minus its last character as the\nlength prefix. That is usually NaN and both slices coerce it to 0, which yields\n[\"\", indexKey] by accident. It stops holding as soon as the truncated input\nparses as a number:\n\n    parseSectionIndexKey(\"12\")   // [\"1\", \"2\"], not [\"\", \"12\"]\n\nA plausible-looking wrong pair is the worst shape for this to fail in. The guard\nreturns [\"\", indexKey] deliberately instead of leaning on NaN propagation, and a\ntest pins it for an old-format key, an arbitrary string, an empty string, and\n\"12\".\n\nThis does not change behavior for any key the function actually produces, since\nthose always hold the separator. It only makes the off-contract behavior\nintentional. I declined a guard one round ago on the grounds of re-landing the\n9.3.0 implementation unchanged; that argument covers the key format, which is\nthe substance, and not an emergent branch that contradicts its own\ndocumentation.\n\nNarrowed the changeset sentence to match. \"Any string it did not produce\" was\nstill overbroad: a malformed input that does hold the separator takes the\narithmetic path and can return a non-empty menu id, so the claim is now scoped\nto inputs without the separator.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a8961eb49860434596c17796fbd9a68a2e762d1d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/trees/a8961eb49860434596c17796fbd9a68a2e762d1d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/commits/779bb3f05b6b711ebb88f2f49d470109e93aa34b","verification":{"payload":"tree a8961eb49860434596c17796fbd9a68a2e762d1d\nparent f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a\nauthor Patrick Lafrance <patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com> 1787795232 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787795232 -0400\n\nfix(react-router): length-prefix the section index key (#689)\n\n* fix(react-router): separate the section index key halves unambiguously\n\nThe section index is keyed by a \"menuId\" and a section \"$id\" joined into one\nstring with a \"-\". Both halves are consumer-provided and either can contain a\n\"-\", so (\"analytics\", \"sidebar-performance\") and (\"analytics-sidebar\",\n\"performance\") produced the same key and shared one index entry.\n\nThat put a nested item in the wrong menu, made registering the second of two\ncolliding sections throw \"A navigation section index has already been\nregistered for the menu\", and let strict mode report one missing section when\ntwo were missing.\n\nThe separator is now NUL, which cannot appear in a \"menuId\" or a section \"$id\"\nanyone would write. Written as an escape rather than a literal control byte, so\nthe file stays plain ASCII and grep does not treat it as binary.\n\n\"parseSectionIndexKey\" splits on the new separator. It stays deprecated because\nthe key format is an implementation detail, but its deprecation note no longer\nclaims the key is unrecoverable — it now returns the correct pair for ids\ncontaining a \"-\", which it could not do before.\n\nTwo existing tests hardcoded \"foo-bar\" as an index key, which is the coupling\nthat deprecation warns against. They now read the pair off the pending items,\nwhere \"menuId\" and \"sectionId\" are separate fields.\n\nThe new tests use the canonical colliding pair rather than random ids. Randomly\ngenerated ids essentially never construct a collision, so a fuzz over a hostile\nalphabet passes against the unfixed code and proves nothing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(react-router): length-prefix the section index key\n\nReview was right that a separator alone is not enough. Nothing constrains a\n\"menuId\" or a section \"$id\" to exclude whichever character is picked, so a\nseparator only moves the collision rather than removing it. A differential over\neight pairs: the \"-\" separator collides twice, and a NUL separator still\ncollides once, on (\"a\" + NUL + \"b\", \"c\") against (\"a\", \"b\" + NUL + \"c\").\nLength-prefixing the menu id collides zero times and round-trips exactly,\nincluding for a menu id that itself starts with a digit and a NUL.\n\nThis is the form that shipped in \"@squide/react-router@9.3.0\" before the 9.4.0\nrevert pulled it back out, so this re-lands it rather than inventing a weaker\nvariant. It answers the \"the invariant is not enforced\" finding by removing the\ninvariant, not by validating input and throwing, which would add a runtime\nfailure mode for ids nobody writes.\n\nAlso from review:\n\n- \"parseSectionIndexKey\" is a public export whose behavior changes here and had\n  no test. Three round-trip tests now drive a key out of the registry and parse\n  it back, covering a \"-\" in an id, the separator in an id, and an empty menu\n  id. They are also the only thing tying it to the private\n  \"createSectionIndexKey\", which nothing else pins.\n- Three comments still described the key as \"menuId-sectionId\". They now name\n  the key builder instead of a literal format.\n- Two tests indexed into a pending registrations array without asserting it was\n  non-empty, so an unexpected empty result failed on a TypeError rather than an\n  assertion. They capture the array and assert its length first.\n- Bumped to minor. The identical fix shipped as a minor in 9.3.0, and the\n  \"getPendingSectionIds\" format change should not reach a consumer pinned to\n  \"~9.4.0\" silently.\n\nDeclined: supporting both the old and new key formats in\n\"parseSectionIndexKey\". Keys are produced and consumed within one bootstrap, so\na key outliving the version that made it is not a real case, and accepting two\nformats reintroduces the ambiguity being removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: state what parseSectionIndexKey actually does with an old-format key\n\nThe changeset said it returns a single element. That was true of the \"split\"\nimplementation it was written against, and I changed the parse to a length\nprefix without re-reading the prose.\n\nAn old-format key holds no separator, so \"indexOf\" is -1, the length prefix\nparses to NaN, and both slices coerce it to 0. Verified:\n\n    parseSectionIndexKey(\"analytics-sidebar-performance\")\n    // [\"\", \"analytics-sidebar-performance\"]\n\nA two-element array with an empty menu id and the whole input as the section\nid, which is worse than a single element because it has the shape of a valid\nresult. Worth stating precisely, since this text is published into two\nCHANGELOG files.\n\nThe behavior itself is unchanged from the implementation released in 9.3.0 and\nthe input is outside the function contract, so this corrects the description\nrather than adding a guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(react-router): make the no-separator path in parseSectionIndexKey explicit\n\nThe path was accidental and my own changeset described it wrongly, which is a\nbug I introduced while fixing the previous review finding.\n\nWithout a guard, an input holding no separator makes \"indexOf\" return -1, so\n\"slice(0, separatorIndex)\" reads the whole input minus its last character as the\nlength prefix. That is usually NaN and both slices coerce it to 0, which yields\n[\"\", indexKey] by accident. It stops holding as soon as the truncated input\nparses as a number:\n\n    parseSectionIndexKey(\"12\")   // [\"1\", \"2\"], not [\"\", \"12\"]\n\nA plausible-looking wrong pair is the worst shape for this to fail in. The guard\nreturns [\"\", indexKey] deliberately instead of leaning on NaN propagation, and a\ntest pins it for an old-format key, an arbitrary string, an empty string, and\n\"12\".\n\nThis does not change behavior for any key the function actually produces, since\nthose always hold the separator. It only makes the off-contract behavior\nintentional. I declined a guard one round ago on the grounds of re-landing the\n9.3.0 implementation unchanged; that argument covers the key format, which is\nthe substance, and not an emergent branch that contradicts its own\ndocumentation.\n\nNarrowed the changeset sentence to match. \"Any string it did not produce\" was\nstill overbroad: a malformed input that does hold the separator takes the\narithmetic path and can return a non-empty menu id, so the claim is now scoped\nto inputs without the separator.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqj5cgCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAbGoQAHSvd68xdgdqhGxqPFtAe+ry\nmuOnmDYGdFIXSwRmqFtDYFA3/kIHC62mOThWOe3hysm3wLonwAr0HvQvsCBTdA0a\noofYQdt9WH8r0o+zwGDIX+LDutbu4xYVPl36OuMK5T0aF1UIOZjRAhHcexkYBKMl\nDi7DkNeow41jm6V3shg78WebBRjhIxmCFyI2+WTlqzKsXFVr2Il21oRkwp8ZTWfB\nkl6xLGxBR+RJUWDR5kaT9SJNq5UNJ7E4uGd+LzOGM0t9GodLW4beID6dn6Sn3EsY\nHPFT5gNYnVSqI8wb1ZqksbwRFx1FloYmhV7PRZH56pDFMm3vNGJmkVpGnqZU3njL\nFCtUVxeP9YgKBraiwo+DUrEHAJSAB3gEQ/Vgk883Ink5AnbYrxL7yHnkQXpMZI15\neFnJufLOVRu1n1PfVYWRIvQmtnNGlgkylpNN6KK8f+2kA+KDwpRfAmHJDic7aKYy\nP1ciy7ZJjVB+rzaBjQ6c06ZFjuRI2iPNJtbih/MjH0xBAgbOCBy4XAX8GfFIdgRs\nQ6Tsg95bQdc6KqIvpwObl177cKGFSBRROM13Ct1Ir+pV75X1lsJ6/Bbo6ypa0NWD\nlAMZAr5Gd3HCTtr4yNUryYFwMbknIHr6XAhQMyL1YBO2SZYM302LNhUAoncpEEzw\nbzyaHmnCiImRWwvF9n7H\n=UyK/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-27T01:47:12Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/workleap/wl-squide/commit/779bb3f05b6b711ebb88f2f49d470109e93aa34b","node_id":"C_kwDOJbZi_NoAKDc3OWJiM2YwNWI2YjcxMWViYjg4ZjJmNDlkNDcwMTA5ZTkzYWEzNGI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/workleap/wl-squide/commit/f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a","sha":"f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a"}],"sha":"779bb3f05b6b711ebb88f2f49d470109e93aa34b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/779bb3f05b6b711ebb88f2f49d470109e93aa34b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/1236702?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/claude","id":209825114,"login":"claude[bot]","node_id":"BOT_kgDODIGtWg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","type":"Bot","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claude%5Bbot%5D","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-26T20:15:28Z","email":"209825114+claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com","name":"claude[bot]"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-26T20:15:28Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore(skill): sync workleap-squide skill (#680)\n\n* chore(skill): sync workleap-squide skill with docs [skip ci]\n\n* chore(skill): bump to 1.19\n\nThis branch was cut from 4d65ac00c and bumped 1.17 -> 1.18, but #681 has\nsince merged and bumped 1.17 -> 1.18 too. Rebased onto main so the sync\nlands on top of #681''s hand-applied $priority corrections rather than\nracing them, and bumped to 1.19 since the merged skill carries two\nindependent rounds of edits.\n\nagent-docs/references/agent-skills.md requires an increment per edit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: claude[bot] <41898282+claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: patrick.lafrance <pa.lafrance@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"715215d924f978ddeb6d989a1e50d1d29820aa7f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/trees/715215d924f978ddeb6d989a1e50d1d29820aa7f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/commits/f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a","verification":{"payload":"tree 715215d924f978ddeb6d989a1e50d1d29820aa7f\nparent 34e98ba651c52f7176bfb4471d1586fa9123c379\nauthor claude[bot] <209825114+claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com> 1787775328 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787775328 -0400\n\nchore(skill): sync workleap-squide skill (#680)\n\n* chore(skill): sync workleap-squide skill with docs [skip ci]\n\n* chore(skill): bump to 1.19\n\nThis branch was cut from 4d65ac00c and bumped 1.17 -> 1.18, but #681 has\nsince merged and bumped 1.17 -> 1.18 too. Rebased onto main so the sync\nlands on top of #681''s hand-applied $priority corrections rather than\nracing them, and bumped to 1.19 since the merged skill carries two\nindependent rounds of edits.\n\nagent-docs/references/agent-skills.md requires an increment per edit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: claude[bot] <41898282+claude[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: patrick.lafrance <pa.lafrance@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqj0lgCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAkWUQAHYr/shUmM5cbnFyKWXzLfpo\nrnGQ5soLoycr/tgNL+3AkRK0YVxwmAOufeR4V2nniH8cPRrQCp3URK/4B2RapaxH\nt5RmFl+QrFrYR72x7eBLi80CFEwXSVmjPMeOVHgOuTs9f2W91/Ml224WX7LK1lG9\ndlvtrEjDadpO+jWyH0eFQve5kSTPG9jNRuyD55fR+gzDekzkgnS344RjKf+Ye8qD\nqQOc/IzJvuWMPK1d9T4vojJrfIFIlymIPkKnIxzVzjTA0xHHCICzoYb1oJ9lwHI+\nAJxlYqrcNR3bwSlQIktlR0JGwuTa1QCIsaxkPIqL0crl6OGO6Z+rkU2/N4MUpWw1\n2bXP9Oc6Vi1Jm+mmNXlkIOjampRBMN/PL3ntzFusODpQphrlcvwwsqViuVPCjPBn\nTkjYLl9LMZw6EFqDyb8Eap05gMmVCUoEB7HkRATTS655anc26Klc4C0aFukG2lD8\nY6QCSqMgvrbPg2MzwYFGfPCXECg2+ecRfFzNNzQeSAK559jPck+FdRRLosxtN5Px\nNYCnslQBa9Iq2m1XxCDsA3eCcd7TJv81g6UdMyNB3F1GegL4f1aeSzSwzPUMrgg1\nbEp6aaxW+uhra2ksA6cin2CvY4NWfTKw9wCQP06rXl2jWH2U0YRXiUv9guZducSb\ntE1e8cvNyLgTk1mIJJeb\n=4QLu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-26T20:15:29Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/workleap/wl-squide/commit/f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a","node_id":"C_kwDOJbZi_NoAKGY1MDYxNzE0NTRmZmZlOGY2ZDlkZjEyYmM1NDE4ZGNmMGU0NTY4OGE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/workleap/wl-squide/commit/34e98ba651c52f7176bfb4471d1586fa9123c379","sha":"34e98ba651c52f7176bfb4471d1586fa9123c379","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/34e98ba651c52f7176bfb4471d1586fa9123c379"}],"sha":"f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/f506171454fffe8f6d9df12bc5418dcf0e45688a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/794579?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/patricklafrance","id":794579,"login":"patricklafrance","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc5NDU3OQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/34e98ba651c52f7176bfb4471d1586fa9123c379/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-26T20:00:20Z","email":"patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Patrick Lafrance"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-26T20:00:20Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs: scope $priority to top-level navigation items (#681)\n\n* docs: scope $priority to top-level navigation items\n\n`$priority` is declared on `RootNavigationItem` only, and\n`useRenderedNavigationItems` sorts just the array it receives —\n`renderItems` recurses into `children` unsorted. The docs promised it\nworked at any depth.\n\nCorrected, without any behavior or type change:\n\n- `FireflyRuntime.md` listed `$priority` under `NavigationLink` and\n  `NavigationSection`, neither of which declares it. Moved to the `item`\n  parameter, where the type is actually `RootNavigationItem`.\n- Added a warning to both \"sorting priority\" sections naming the three\n  ways to nest an item: an inline `children` literal is a `TS2353`\n  error, while a variable carrying a `$priority` and the `sectionId`\n  option both compile and drop it silently.\n- `useRenderedNavigationItems.md`, `useNavigationItems.md` and\n  `useNavigationItemsByMenu.md` omitted `$priority` entirely and named\n  the wrong array element type.\n- `agent-docs/design/routing-and-navigation.md` claimed the hook\n  \"applies `$canRender`, sorting by `$priority`\". It sorts top-level\n  items only, and never executes `$canRender` — it forwards it to the\n  layout as `canRender`, which the public docs already document.\n- The skill''s `registerNavigationItem` example paired `$priority` with\n  `sectionId` in one call, teaching the combination that silently drops\n  the priority. Split into two examples.\n- Fixed three stale links found in the same sections: a hardcoded\n  `localhost:5000` target, an empty `[useRenderedNavigationItems]()`,\n  and a \"nested routes\" link pointing at its own heading.\n\nNo changeset: docs-only, no package source change, and the docs site\npublishes from `main` on every push.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: address review on the $priority scoping\n\n- \"cannot be set on a nested item\" was false at runtime and contradicted\n  the warning this PR adds elsewhere. `#addNestedItem` pushes the\n  caller''s object verbatim into `parentSection.item.children`\n  (`NavigationItemRegistry.ts:364-367`), so an item nested through the\n  `sectionId` option really does carry its `$priority` in the tree these\n  hooks return. It is ignored, not absent — and this matters most in\n  these two docs, whose audience renders a menu by hand.\n- The skill still named the wrong element type for both nav item hooks\n  and for `getNavigationItemsByMenu`, which is the same inaccuracy this\n  PR fixes in the public docs, in the copy agents actually load.\n- Named the option `$priority` rather than \"priority\" in the modular\n  tabs recipe, and stopped calling a tab \"him\".\n- \"section be specifying\" -> \"by specifying\", in both docs carrying the\n  identical sentence rather than only the one flagged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(skill): nested items can be ordered, just not by $priority\n\n\"There is no supported way to do that today\" overstated it and\ncontradicted patterns.md in this same PR, which says a nested item\nrenders in the order it appears in its section''s children array.\n\nOrdering nested items is supported — the lever is array order, which for\nsectionId registrations is registration order. Only $priority does not\napply. An agent reading runtime-api.md alone would otherwise tell a\nconsumer nested items cannot be ordered at all, which is the same class\nof wrong answer this PR exists to fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(skill): give the nested example its own id\n\nThe nested-item example reused \"page-id\" and \"/page\" from the top-level\nexample directly above it, in the same fenced block. The block reads as\none sequence of calls, so an agent copying it registers two items with\nthe same $id in the root menu.\n\nNothing rejects that — the registry only throws on duplicate identifiers\nfor sections, so both links reach useRenderedNavigationItems, which uses\n$id as the React key. The third example in the block already uses a\ndistinct id, so this was the odd one out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c6a74fd29f97b422e52588b466c582fc9e405f52","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/trees/c6a74fd29f97b422e52588b466c582fc9e405f52"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/commits/34e98ba651c52f7176bfb4471d1586fa9123c379","verification":{"payload":"tree c6a74fd29f97b422e52588b466c582fc9e405f52\nparent 2014eb210c02101bde20daf9a311e71fc4d2d83c\nauthor Patrick Lafrance <patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com> 1787774420 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787774420 -0400\n\ndocs: scope $priority to top-level navigation items (#681)\n\n* docs: scope $priority to top-level navigation items\n\n`$priority` is declared on `RootNavigationItem` only, and\n`useRenderedNavigationItems` sorts just the array it receives —\n`renderItems` recurses into `children` unsorted. The docs promised it\nworked at any depth.\n\nCorrected, without any behavior or type change:\n\n- `FireflyRuntime.md` listed `$priority` under `NavigationLink` and\n  `NavigationSection`, neither of which declares it. Moved to the `item`\n  parameter, where the type is actually `RootNavigationItem`.\n- Added a warning to both \"sorting priority\" sections naming the three\n  ways to nest an item: an inline `children` literal is a `TS2353`\n  error, while a variable carrying a `$priority` and the `sectionId`\n  option both compile and drop it silently.\n- `useRenderedNavigationItems.md`, `useNavigationItems.md` and\n  `useNavigationItemsByMenu.md` omitted `$priority` entirely and named\n  the wrong array element type.\n- `agent-docs/design/routing-and-navigation.md` claimed the hook\n  \"applies `$canRender`, sorting by `$priority`\". It sorts top-level\n  items only, and never executes `$canRender` — it forwards it to the\n  layout as `canRender`, which the public docs already document.\n- The skill''s `registerNavigationItem` example paired `$priority` with\n  `sectionId` in one call, teaching the combination that silently drops\n  the priority. Split into two examples.\n- Fixed three stale links found in the same sections: a hardcoded\n  `localhost:5000` target, an empty `[useRenderedNavigationItems]()`,\n  and a \"nested routes\" link pointing at its own heading.\n\nNo changeset: docs-only, no package source change, and the docs site\npublishes from `main` on every push.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: address review on the $priority scoping\n\n- \"cannot be set on a nested item\" was false at runtime and contradicted\n  the warning this PR adds elsewhere. `#addNestedItem` pushes the\n  caller''s object verbatim into `parentSection.item.children`\n  (`NavigationItemRegistry.ts:364-367`), so an item nested through the\n  `sectionId` option really does carry its `$priority` in the tree these\n  hooks return. It is ignored, not absent — and this matters most in\n  these two docs, whose audience renders a menu by hand.\n- The skill still named the wrong element type for both nav item hooks\n  and for `getNavigationItemsByMenu`, which is the same inaccuracy this\n  PR fixes in the public docs, in the copy agents actually load.\n- Named the option `$priority` rather than \"priority\" in the modular\n  tabs recipe, and stopped calling a tab \"him\".\n- \"section be specifying\" -> \"by specifying\", in both docs carrying the\n  identical sentence rather than only the one flagged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(skill): nested items can be ordered, just not by $priority\n\n\"There is no supported way to do that today\" overstated it and\ncontradicted patterns.md in this same PR, which says a nested item\nrenders in the order it appears in its section''s children array.\n\nOrdering nested items is supported — the lever is array order, which for\nsectionId registrations is registration order. Only $priority does not\napply. An agent reading runtime-api.md alone would otherwise tell a\nconsumer nested items cannot be ordered at all, which is the same class\nof wrong answer this PR exists to fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(skill): give the nested example its own id\n\nThe nested-item example reused \"page-id\" and \"/page\" from the top-level\nexample directly above it, in the same fenced block. The block reads as\none sequence of calls, so an agent copying it registers two items with\nthe same $id in the root menu.\n\nNothing rejects that — the registry only throws on duplicate identifiers\nfor sections, so both links reach useRenderedNavigationItems, which uses\n$id as the React key. The third example in the block already uses a\ndistinct id, so this was the odd one out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqj0XUCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAr1wQAEp4fAWXz0RUTRGjcpBw8lhX\nWGfuOIiWqHkHOCFfooUWDHlgKKZ7NNKzGozca4yldvV3Tui+UTAYIQV2JTr+GIik\naUBBdCg4t6tRkH17em9SzArDlTIvgrtCbMDOCJ8HbGBJNw9r550JChoSSwi6DlfR\nD1No46qOiOOHTY/i/pxxU4s8UZsbL1qIwG5nHEHkN1Bp2sJYu7EL10DG6QqOlXc7\nb3Yyfus7t/trt/WBuFI3MDJQKKd2DsrX14xXMuHnAVIZqN8K67FV8yATH5UUn0ZR\nJKmCsZSScBBG04vvYf+/E0pWyER3mXEr9AQ/GI+6qIVbfsYA/KWbM3eI+SYxePg7\nbR7n3iWw0FWijFpAY+hUz3BT96XyP1uWy8lfbiAApNr3Cjq8oIJ0PioZzLlPcWZo\nAqSTvQBGAS7ycvmk132qBA6pPjpWyQ4MuoVqWfWiJyAOmEgFEzxSiPds/S199BVm\npKD/JLXmq2GaJJlJnR+OORYhMkB3Z5QuzgZBmRhSmvaQEe3Gtdc5Z1oj74mpGE9L\n80n8IRDch6LQFEX5ZnhyrrEYtEixQDh87HiLVwyuulNTIu+i0LJiivr03rF/hHjB\nlKyTZHniSrm7AOJY3iKXLEQSeRESSd+EYclITQTDYFMTOFlwJ8aWwlRZ/WL/7QNH\nBSNzwWAT0cME3wYzuEYc\n=n//L\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-26T20:00:21Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/we