hub.com/users/patricklafrance/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/patricklafrance","id":794579,"login":"patricklafrance","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc5NDU3OQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/patricklafrance","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/commits/fb2669e985e20d71363d50d6d703033afaf1c7a9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-25T13:54:19Z","email":"patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Patrick Lafrance"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-25T13:54:19Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: registration reporting and navigation item correctness (#658) (#671)\n\n* fix(react-router): section index key ambiguity across menus\n\nA navigation section is indexed by its \"menuId\" and its \"$id\" joined with a \"-\",\nso (\"analytics\", \"sidebar-performance\") and (\"analytics-sidebar\", \"performance\")\nproduced the same key. Two distinct sections of two distinct menus shared one\nindex entry, which:\n\n- attached a nested item registered with a \"sectionId\" to the other menu''s\n  section,\n- threw \"A navigation section index has already been registered for the menu\"\n  when both sections were registered,\n- reported one missing section instead of two in strict mode, naming only one of\n  the two pairs.\n\nKeys are now joined with a separator that cannot appear in an id.\nparseSectionIndexKey round-trips correctly again but stays deprecated, the key\nformat is not part of the public contract. getPendingSectionIds gains a note\nsaying its keys are opaque, and the two tests that asserted on the literal\n\"foo-bar\" key now assert on the \"menuId\" and \"sectionId\" carried by the pending\nitems instead, so they no longer pin an internal format.\n\nRaised by review on #667. The suggested patch there concatenated without any\nseparator at all, which is strictly more ambiguous, so the diagnosis was taken\nand the patch was not.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(react-router): address review on the section index key fix\n\n- Corrected the last comment still documenting the removed \"menuId-sectionId\"\n  format, inside clearDeferredItems, the function whose single-pass cleanup\n  depends on the key layout.\n- The strict mode message counts sections, not items, so it now says\n  \"navigation section\". The new test asserts the section names rather than only\n  the count, which is what the collision actually broke: both pending items\n  shared one bucket, so only one of the two pairs was ever named.\n- Added the reverse registration order. Registering the pending item before the\n  colliding section went through a different branch and produced a worse\n  symptom: the item was completed into the other menu''s section and the pending\n  bucket was emptied, so validation stopped reporting the missing section\n  entirely.\n- Added a static/deferred collision across clearDeferredItems. The two pending\n  items shared a bucket, the static one survived the clear under the deferred\n  section''s key, and the replay threw the registration type guard for two\n  unrelated registrations.\n- Added a parseSectionIndexKey round-trip. It had no coverage at all, and\n  nothing in the framework calls it, so it could drift away from\n  createSectionIndexKey without any test noticing.\n- Renamed the first test, which promised a positive attachment it does not\n  assert, and moved the duplicate section test to the registry suite since it\n  passes no sectionId and exercises #addSectionIndex directly.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(react-router): report and validate deferred registration update runs\n\nThree related changes from #658, each reversing a behaviour that was\ndeliberately written, so they carry two ADRs rather than shipping as fixes.\n\nThe registry owns its copy of deferred items (ADR-0023). A nested item is\nattached by pushing onto the \"children\" array of the indexed section, which was\nthe object the module passed in. A module holding its section at module scope\naccumulated one child per update run, rendering three copies of a single link\nafter two flag flips. This is the defect behind the downstream report in #658,\nand none of the fixes that issue lists address it. Cloning is narrowed to the\ndeferred path, since the static phase runs once and cannot accumulate, and it\ncopies property descriptors so prototypes survive and getters stay lazy.\n\nUpdate runs report the real outcome (ADR-0022). The transactional scope declared\n\"registered\" before the replay had happened, and the replay itself logged\nnothing, so a menu could lose a link while the console showed a green success.\nThe status union gained \"buffered\", complete() returns the replay results, and\ncompleteDeferredRegistrationScope reports them through the same function that\nreports a static registration.\n\nNavigation validation re-runs after an update (ADR-0022). It ran once, off a\none-shot modules-ready listener the update path never re-notified, so a lost\nsection surfaced only on a page reload. useStrictRegistrationMode now\nre-validates on the update-completed event. The counter and effect are\ndeliberate: the event is dispatched inside an async callback, so throwing from\nthe listener would be an unhandled rejection rather than a React error.\n_validateRegistrations gained includeRoutes, since routes are frozen after\nphase 1 and re-validating them could only re-throw a bootstrap misconfiguration\non every flag flip.\n\nADR-0006''s claim that strict mode catches misconfigured parents is amended\nrather than superseded, the queue decision itself is not reversed.\n\nDocuments strictMode and the update-run idempotency rule, neither of which had\nany coverage in docs/.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(react-router): address review on the update run reporting\n\nThe clone no longer throws on a frozen section. Copying descriptors copies\n\"writable: false\" and accessor descriptors faithfully, so assigning\nclone.children afterwards threw on a frozen section and on a class exposing\nchildren through a getter. The new array is built into the descriptor bag\ninstead. This was a regression, not a limitation: such a section previously\nthrew only when something was actually nested under it, whereas the assignment\nthrew for every deferred section including leaves.\n\nADR-0022 claimed the registration status union was \"unreachable by consumers\".\nThat is false. index.ts re-exports all of NavigationItemRegistry.ts and firefly\nre-exports that, so the registry, both scope classes and the result type are\nimportable from @squide/firefly, and the scope''s _registry field is public. The\nbump stays minor, but on the honest grounds: exported undocumented internals\nthat no plausible consumer narrows on, and a compile error rather than silent\nbreakage if one does.\n\nTwo consequences ADR-0022 was missing. An update run that rejects is never\nre-validated even though its deferred state was already cleared, because the\ncompleted event is dispatched after the await. And a static pending\nregistration tolerated since bootstrap is now re-reported on every update run.\n\nThe docs snippet had the deferred registration function arguments backwards,\nwhich would have thrown for anyone who copied it, and showed only an\nanti-pattern. It now shows the recommended shape. The copy claim is narrowed to\nsections, links are handed back by reference so mutating one still shows\nthrough. strictMode also suppresses the throw when the active location matches\nno route, which neither the property list nor the usage section mentioned. Both\nline highlight annotations pointed one line off.\n\nFive tests for behaviour that was claimed but unpinned: the clone''s recursion\ninto nested sections, prototype preservation, the frozen section case,\nproduction logging the report rather than throwing it, and the accumulation fix\ndriven end to end through createDeferredRegistrationsRunner. That last one\nmattered: every existing runner fixture allocates a fresh literal per run, so\nthe whole firefly suite passed with the clone deleted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(react-router): address bot review on the section index key\n\nLength-prefix the menu id in a section index key. Nothing constrains a\nmenuId or a section $id to exclude the separator, so joining with a\nseparator alone moved the collision rather than removing it: (\"a\\0b\",\n\"c\") and (\"a\", \"b\\0c\") still produced the same key. Prefixing the length\nmakes the split unambiguous whatever the ids hold, and\nparseSectionIndexKey reads the menu id back with it.\n\nNarrow the clone''s class-instance claim. Property descriptors preserve\nthe prototype, instanceof and lazy getters, but ECMAScript private\nfields are slots rather than properties, so a section whose accessor\nreads a #private field throws on the clone where the original instance\nworked. TypeScript''s private compiles to an ordinary property and is\nunaffected. Verified both cases rather than assumed. Recorded in\nADR-0023 as an accepted, narrow regression.\n\nFix the update-run docs snippet. It destructured featureFlags off the\ndeferred data argument, which is unknown for\nModuleRegisterFunction<FireflyRuntime> and would not compile, and it\nregistered a section with no children, which renders nothing. It now\nmatches the feature flag example on the same page.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2846f84ee4939143983dceae76c74aef3134026b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/trees/2846f84ee4939143983dceae76c74aef3134026b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/workleap/wl-squide/git/commits/fb2669e985e20d71363d50d6d703033afaf1c7a9","verification":{"payload":"tree 2846f84ee4939143983dceae76c74aef3134026b\nparent 23c6bfb6a14f109369b882d6dea24b7db19a7c05\nauthor Patrick Lafrance <patricklafrance@users.noreply.github.com> 1787666059 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787666059 -0400\n\nfix: registration reporting and navigation item correctness (#658) (#671)\n\n* fix(react-router): section index key ambiguity across menus\n\nA navigation section is indexed by its \"menuId\" and its \"$id\" joined with a \"-\",\nso (\"analytics\", \"sidebar-performance\") and (\"analytics-sidebar\", \"performance\")\nproduced the same key. Two distinct sections of two distinct menus shared one\nindex entry, which:\n\n- attached a nested item registered with a \"sectionId\" to the other menu''s\n  section,\n- threw \"A navigation section index has already been registered for the menu\"\n  when both sections were registered,\n- reported one missing section instead of two in strict mode, naming only one of\n  the two pairs.\n\nKeys are now joined with a separator that cannot appear in an id.\nparseSectionIndexKey round-trips correctly again but stays deprecated, the key\nformat is not part of the public contract. getPendingSectionIds gains a note\nsaying its keys are opaque, and the two tests that asserted on the literal\n\"foo-bar\" key now assert on the \"menuId\" and \"sectionId\" carried by the pending\nitems instead, so they no longer pin an internal format.\n\nRaised by review on #667. The suggested patch there concatenated without any\nseparator at all, which is strictly more ambiguous, so the diagnosis was taken\nand the patch was not.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(react-router): address review on the section index key fix\n\n- Corrected the last comment still documenting the removed \"menuId-sectionId\"\n  format, inside clearDeferredItems, the function whose single-pass cleanup\n  depends on the key layout.\n- The strict mode message counts sections, not items, so it now says\n  \"navigation section\". The new test asserts the section names rather than only\n  the count, which is what the collision actually broke: both pending items\n  shared one bucket, so only one of the two pairs was ever named.\n- Added the reverse registration order. Registering the pending item before the\n  colliding section went through a different branch and produced a worse\n  symptom: the item was completed into the other menu''s section and the pending\n  bucket was emptied, so validation stopped reporting the missing section\n  entirely.\n- Added a static/deferred collision across clearDeferredItems. The two pending\n  items shared a bucket, the static one survived the clear under the deferred\n  section''s key, and the replay threw the registration type guard for two\n  unrelated registrations.\n- Added a parseSectionIndexKey round-trip. It had no coverage at all, and\n  nothing in the framework calls it, so it could drift away from\n  createSectionIndexKey without any test noticing.\n- Renamed the first test, which promised a positive attachment it does not\n  assert, and moved the duplicate section test to the registry suite since it\n  passes no sectionId and exercises #addSectionIndex directly.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(react-router): report and validate deferred registration update runs\n\nThree related changes from #658, each reversing a behaviour that was\ndeliberately written, so they carry two ADRs rather than shipping as fixes.\n\nThe registry owns its copy of deferred items (ADR-0023). A nested item is\nattached by pushing onto the \"children\" array of the indexed section, which was\nthe object the module passed in. A module holding its section at module scope\naccumulated one child per update run, rendering three copies of a single link\nafter two flag flips. This is the defect behind the downstream report in #658,\nand none of the fixes that issue lists address it. Cloning is narrowed to the\ndeferred path, since the static phase runs once and cannot accumulate, and it\ncopies property descriptors so prototypes survive and getters stay lazy.\n\nUpdate runs report the real outcome (ADR-0022). The transactional scope declared\n\"registered\" before the replay had happened, and the replay itself logged\nnothing, so a menu could lose a link while the console showed a green success.\nThe status union gained \"buffered\", complete() returns the replay results, and\ncompleteDeferredRegistrationScope reports them through the same function that\nreports a static registration.\n\nNavigation validation re-runs after an update (ADR-0022). It ran once, off a\none-shot modules-ready listener the update path never re-notified, so a lost\nsection surfaced only on a page reload. useStrictRegistrationMode now\nre-validates on the update-completed event. The counter and effect are\ndeliberate: the event is dispatched inside an async callback, so throwing from\nthe listener would be an unhandled rejection rather than a React error.\n_validateRegistrations gained includeRoutes, since routes are frozen after\nphase 1 and re-validating them could only re-throw a bootstrap misconfiguration\non every flag flip.\n\nADR-0006''s claim that strict mode catches misconfigured parents is amended\nrather than superseded, the queue decision itself is not reversed.\n\nDocuments strictMode and the update-run idempotency rule, neither of which had\nany coverage in docs/.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(react-router): address review on the update run reporting\n\nThe clone no longer throws on a frozen section. Copying descriptors copies\n\"writable: false\" and accessor descriptors faithfully, so assigning\nclone.children afterwards threw on a frozen section and on a class exposing\nchildren through a getter. The new array is built into the descriptor bag\ninstead. This was a regression, not a limitation: such a section previously\nthrew only when something was actually nested under it, whereas the assignment\nthrew for every deferred section including leaves.\n\nADR-0022 claimed the registration status union was \"unreachable by consumers\".\nThat is false. index.ts re-exports all of NavigationItemRegistry.ts and firefly\nre-exports that, so the registry, both scope classes and the result type are\nimportable from @squide/firefly, and the scope''s _registry field is public. The\nbump stays minor, but on the honest grounds: exported undocumented internals\nthat no plausible consumer narrows on, and a compile error rather than silent\nbreakage if one does.\n\nTwo consequences ADR-0022 was missing. An update run that rejects is never\nre-validated even though its deferred state was already cleared, because the\ncompleted event is dispatched after the await. And a static pending\nregistration tolerated since bootstrap is now re-reported on every update run.\n\nThe docs snippet had the deferred registration function arguments backwards,\nwhich would have thrown for anyone who copied it, and showed only an\nanti-pattern. It now shows the recommended shape. The copy claim is narrowed to\nsections, links are handed back by reference so mutating one still shows\nthrough. strictMode also suppresses the throw when the active location matches\nno route, which neither the property list nor the usage section mentioned. Both\nline highlight annotations pointed one line off.\n\nFive tests for behaviour that was claimed but unpinned: the clone''s recursion\ninto nested sections, prototype preservation, the frozen section case,\nproduction logging the report rather than throwing it, and the accumulation fix\ndriven end to end through createDeferredRegistrationsRunner. That last one\nmattered: every existing runner fixture allocates a fresh literal per run, so\nthe whole firefly suite passed with the clone deleted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(react-router): address bot review on the section index key\n\nLength-prefix the menu id in a section index key. Nothing constrains a\nmenuId or a section $id to exclude the separator, so joining with a\nseparator alone moved the collision rather than removing it: (\"a\\0b\",\n\"c\") and (\"a\", \"b\\0c\") still produced the same key. Prefixing the length\nmakes the split unambiguous whatever the ids hold, and\nparseSectionIndexKey reads the menu id back with it.\n\nNarrow the clone''s class-instance claim. Property descriptors preserve\nthe prototype, instanceof and lazy getters, but ECMAScript private\nfields are slots rather than properties, so a section whose accessor\nreads a #private field throws on the clone where the original instance\nworked. TypeScript''s private compiles to an ordinary property and is\nunaffected. Verified both cases rather than assumed. Recorded in\nADR-0023 as an accepted, narrow regression.\n\nFix the update-run docs snippet. It destructured featureFlags off the\ndeferred data argument, which is unknown for\nModuleRegisterFunction<FireflyRuntime> and would not compile, and it\nregistered a section with no children, which renders nothing. It now\nmatches the feature flag example on the same page.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqjZ6LCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4PAQAKaD8NS0v7LpUBhD8kzWy+HC\ntiHoGM25JcY0XlW7Xa2k1Ocpkm0/gVCbS4uP/wYmLV27EKOfA8xrK2My/Ps4jeI2\nuLVVbfY+1Gx1f2EQlR6xsa6CgWHtPQrlwIu48cpgbB2P2XzmLTV08zqqhZs00zQO\nhkV5i4agUsRvV2F5g4Aj+wj7YyhNcSNxr9PJBFMJ2QepWIsmg5M2sL/tL2Bq+Ndi\nc0y1ftEg226JY0pYpuqiysR1Y/Hsl7yAgSthWilqXfo8Co/YqJIa+5oSiQI6XJ9W\n1ynEarjE5B5/DUv5uDTJDCr+jz3tsb+HJ05wFePFVSFKepemxubgAeZdRteDsB8r\nV7DZ9ajer3/NgRv01X/ZpT5wDlrWpGz2JVFFik/ajxpv74fx04KC5iIGxpRnstlD\nhMTzAHU/gWiu/I5DdxNKOeHdB8ZacsU84HEkKu8xtp2vDjbv5XUu+rDUbjHTMz9l\nqhd2d4QbGJPPPJho161+nOVGXNxkMp8Q2EUsGu/3wYuBMmmVbrbPte58aEL8ejpH\n/ZoeysQugRYSs4lcQ4zURkNqtk2DLr/tLu640c2dxnrlQybOvhLhg9dDbbpFD/qL\nY93QYLBTgUW/BxHddywFXGIDH/NeuPAV2e/fwTjCw2BFvYSO1EFwn3a/T5/VOswZ\nCAy9sFjkl62y6A7BDkdm\n=U3gd\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-25T13:54:20Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/workleap/wl-squide/commi